## Research question

Can Symtropy certify that a WPT system remains energy-consistent and passive at the instant it switches between compensation topologies, resonant modes, active coil sets, receivers, or roadway segments—even when the state dimension changes?

## Why this is a real gap

The existing workstreams qualify frozen real-valued inductance matrices (#3530), frequency-dependent network passivity (#3546), transient waveforms and reduced-model error (#3572), segment/route energy accounting (#3587), and handover authorization and communication safety (#3517). Those checks do not by themselves certify the jump map between two different circuit topologies.

A pair of modes can each be passive in steady operation while an inconsistent state remap at the switching instant silently creates, destroys, duplicates or loses stored electromagnetic energy. A frequency-grid passivity result is not a hybrid transition certificate.

This gap is operationally relevant. Li et al. (IEEE Transactions on Transportation Electrification, early access 12 June 2026, DOI [10.1109/TTE.2026.3703005](https://doi.org/10.1109/TTE.2026.3703005)) experimentally demonstrate a 500 W AGV WPT prototype switching between LCC-S and LCC-S-S modes with coupling reversal. The study reports 90.28% maximum DC-DC efficiency and an expanded misalignment range. This is a transition-design reference, not a matched EV-power benchmark.

The theoretical foundations are established rather than novel: Frasca et al. (IEEE TCAS-I, 2010, DOI [10.1109/TCSI.2010.2052511](https://doi.org/10.1109/TCSI.2010.2052511)) study passive switched electrical networks and energy-minimizing state reinitialization; Restrepo et al. (Automatica, November 2025, DOI [10.1016/j.automatica.2025.112496](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.automatica.2025.112496)) develop passivity-preserving energy tanks for switched systems with state-dimension changes. Our contribution must be a WPT-specific, source-bound transition contract and falsifiable benchmark—not a claim to invent hybrid passivity.

## Paradigm shift: a mode transition is an energy event

Represent each discrete mode q with its own state space and storage/Hamiltonian function H_q(x_q). During continuous evolution, each mode must provide its own passivity or energy-balance evidence. At a switch q- to q+, retain:
- pre-switch state and stored energy;
- post-switch state and stored energy;
- an explicit state/charge/flux mapping or documented physical reinitialization rule;
- signed energy supplied or extracted by switching/control action;
- nonnegative event dissipation;
- source and load port-energy increments over the transition window;
- measurement/model uncertainty and mode/topology digests.

For a declared switching-event energy boundary, use:

    Delta H = W_switch,net - E_event,dissipated + residual

Positive W_switch,net means external work supplied into the modeled storage. The validator must report the residual and its uncertainty; it must not infer an unmeasured source from a positive residual.

For an ideal transition with no external work, a passive state remap must not increase stored energy beyond declared numerical and measurement uncertainty. If a controller uses an energy tank or other buffer to permit a jump, the tank's pre/post state and energy debit/credit must be included explicitly. A mode's PASSIVE label cannot compensate for a non-passive transition.

This is an event-level contract. It does not replace continuous-time positive-realness, full-matrix passivity, transient stability, safety, or metrology.

## Baselines to compare

1. Naive state-vector copy or truncation at mode change (negative control).
2. Static per-mode passivity only, with no transition certificate (negative control).
3. Charge/flux-consistent, energy-minimizing reinitialization for switched RLC networks.
4. A hybrid energy-tank transition policy with explicit buffer state and energy debit.
5. A WPT-specific state mapping for SS/LCC/LCC-S/LCC-S-S, multi-frequency branches, coil/segment activation and receiver entry/exit.

Do not compare only output-voltage continuity. Track stored energy, source work, dissipated energy, current/voltage stress and per-receiver service.

## Frozen known-truth scenarios

- same topology, identity mapping, conserved storage;
- dimension increase/decrease with a physically declared charge/flux mapping;
- passive transition with bounded positive dissipation;
- transition with externally supplied switching work that exactly explains the storage increase;
- positive storage jump with zero declared work (must reject);
- energy-tank-supported transition with sufficient and insufficient tank reserve;
- both adjacent modes individually passive but an incorrect jump map injects energy;
- omitted inductor/capacitor state (must not disappear from the ledger);
- duplicate, reordered or overlapping switch events;
- nonfinite/negative dissipation, inconsistent units, mismatched topology digest;
- uncertainty interval overlapping the energy-creation boundary (UNRESOLVED, never PASS);
- source/load energy crossing the event window, counted once and not hidden inside switch work.

Include seeded random positive-definite storage matrices and known-truth state maps. Keep synthetic circuit fixtures distinct from FEM or measured WPT evidence.

## Metrics

- event energy residual and conservative residual interval;
- pre/post storage and energy-tank reserve;
- false-pass / false-reject counts;
- state/charge/flux constraint residuals;
- per-mode and transition certificate coverage;
- peak and RMS current/voltage at transition;
- transient output overshoot, settling time and interruption;
- handover interruption energy and route-level Wh impact;
- event-evaluation latency and deterministic replay;
- fraction of transitions that correctly abstain or de-energize due to missing evidence.

## Proposed targets (research goals, not standards or achieved results)

- zero false PASS on frozen energy-injecting negative controls;
- zero unaccounted state-energy deletion across dimension-changing transitions;
- 100% of mode changes require a passing transition certificate or explicit safe-abort disposition;
- energy-event residual <=0.5% of pre-event stored energy on deterministic synthetic cases away from zero-crossing boundaries; otherwise UNRESOLVED;
- uncertainty-aware classification with no false PASS as uncertainty expands;
- energy-tank reserve never negative and all withdrawals explicitly metered;
- no route-service continuity claim unless transition waveforms and segment ledger both support it.

Targets must be refined against a documented reference implementation and measurement resolution. They are not IEC/ISO thresholds.

## Deliverables

- versioned JSON Schema for hybrid mode-transition records;
- deterministic known-truth JSONL corpus with positive and negative controls;
- dependency-light Rust evaluator and CLI;
- independent numerical oracle and explicit error budget;
- exact-head CI and reproducible example output;
- integration contract linking each transition to its upstream mode/model certificate and downstream segment/r
